ldings without the need for extensive excavation [Data: Entities (190, 196)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Applications of ROTA SUBTERRÂNEA and Associated Structures\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'ROTA SUBTERRÂNEA' method, along with associated structures like 'TÚNEIS OU GALERIAS' (tunnels or galleries), is employed in environments demanding a high degree of protection for cabling infrastructure. These underground solutions are essential in dense urban areas where robust, protected infrastructure is a necessity due to space constraints and the potential for disruption from surface-level activities [Data: Entities (188, 198)]. Large industrial complexes and university campuses also benefit from tunnels or galleries, which can house extensive cabling networks, power, and other utilities, providing a centralized and protected means of distribution [Data: Entities (191, 197)]. The 'FACILIDADE DE ENTRADA' (entry point) for external telecommunication services often integrates with 'ROTA SUBTERRÂNEA', ensuring that the transition from external to internal infrastructure is secure and protected [Data: Entities (192, 204)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Horizontal Cabling and its Role in Connectivity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Horizontal cabling is a critical component of any structured cabling system, forming the link between the telecommunications room and the work area outlets. Within this community, 'CABO HORIZONTAL' is directly associated with the 'SISTEMA DE CABEAMENTO ESTRUTURADO', emphasizing its foundational role in network connectivity [Data: Relationships (2723)]. This cabling connects workstations, access points, and other end devices to the broader network infrastructure, terminating in the 'CENTRO DE TELECOMUNICACOES' (telecommunications center) or 'SALA DE EQUIPAMENTOS' (equipment room) [Data: Entities (1994), Relationships (534)]. The quality and proper installation of horizontal cabling are cruc